{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Derivative.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Derivative.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"derivative derivative","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Derivative.md","fileName":"Derivative.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Derivative","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Optimization and Differentiation]] · [[Differential Calculus]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Optimization and Differentiation","Differential Calculus","Partial Derivative","algorithms.epub","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf","foundationsofmath.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Derivative.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Derivative.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"derivative derivative","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Derivative.md","fileName":"Derivative.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Derivative","heading":"Derivative","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A derivative describes the rate at which a function changes at a particular point. Geometrically, it is the slope of the tangent line at that point.\n\nIts sign provides local directional information. If increasing a parameter produces a negative derivative of loss, the loss decreases; if the derivative is positive, increasing that parameter raises the loss. A multivariable function has a [[Partial Derivative]] for each variable.\n\nFormally, the derivative is the limit of the difference quotient $(f(x+h)-f(x))/h$ as $h$ approaches zero, when that limit exists. Higher derivatives describe changes in earlier derivatives, and the second derivative supplies curvature information for identifying local extrema.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Optimization and Differentiation","Differential Calculus","Partial Derivative","algorithms.epub","